No. Even though the warp starts converged, `__activemask()` inside the branch is only a snapshot of currently active lanes, not a guarantee that all 19 branch participants are present. On Volta or newer, a caller’s mask can exclude lane 18, making the requested source value undefined.

For `__shfl_sync`, each caller must have its own bit set in the mask, and every non-exited thread named in that mask must execute the corresponding intrinsic with the same mask. The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle; `width = 32` makes source index 18 refer to physical lane 18, but does not make that lane participate. The intrinsic synchronizes the named participants for register exchange, not memory ordering.

Compute the participation mask before diverging, then use that fixed mask. This device function requires every lane of a full physical warp to call it at the converged point; each lane supplies its own float. Lanes 0–18 receive lane 18’s float, while lanes 19–31 retain their own value and never call the shuffle.

```cpp
__device__ float read_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned int thread_rank =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = thread_rank % 32u;
    const bool participates = lane <= 18u;
    const unsigned int mask =
        __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, participates);

    if (participates) {
        value = __shfl_sync(mask, value, 18, 32);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Compile as CUDA device code and call only from complete warps, with all 32 lanes reaching the ballot. The ballot produces `0x0007ffff`; all 19 named lanes execute the same shuffle, including source lane 18. No separate `__syncwarp()`, shared memory, or atomics are needed.

Use five reduction shuffles and one broadcast, with a fixed mask naming exactly lanes 0–18. Every caller executes all six shuffles; a lane without a partner reads itself but skips the addition.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    const unsigned int rank =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = rank % 32u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    float sum = value;
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const unsigned int partner = lane + offset;
        const bool has_partner = partner < 19u;
        const int source = has_partner
            ? static_cast<int>(partner)
            : static_cast<int>(lane);

        const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            sum = sum + other;
        }
    }
    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, 0, 32);
}
```

Usage contract: exactly physical lanes 0–18 of the same warp call `sum19` once for this collective operation, each supplying its float. Lanes 19–31 must not call it. No full-warp ballot or implicit lockstep is required.

**Addition order and proof:** Write the original inputs as `a[i]`. Initially lane `i` holds only `a[i]`. Process offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 in that order, always adding the partner’s pre-stage sum to the caller’s pre-stage sum. After the stage with offset `d`, lane `i` contains exactly the inputs at indices `i + k*d < 19`, with the nesting determined by these successive additions. Before that stage, its own sum contains the even multiples of `d`, while the partner’s sum contains the odd multiples. These sets are disjoint and together give the claimed set; if the partner is absent, the odd set is empty. Thus after offset 1, lane 0 contains every input exactly once. The sixth shuffle broadcasts that result to every caller.

**Collective validity:** Every caller’s bit is in `mask`, and all 19 named lanes execute each corresponding shuffle with the same mask and width. Every source is either a partner below 19, the caller itself, or lane 0, so every source participates in that same collective. Partnerless lanes still execute the shuffle. The `_sync` collectives provide the required rendezvous despite independent thread scheduling; this reasoning does not rely on implicit lockstep. Floating-point rounding follows the stated tree order; finite inputs can still overflow during addition.

Use a fold, a 16-lane butterfly all-reduction, and a three-source redistribution. Every caller executes six float shuffles; the last shuffle does not broadcast one lane’s completed sum.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    const unsigned int rank =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = rank % 32u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    // Fold inputs 16, 17, 18 into lanes 0, 1, 2.
    const int fold_source = static_cast<int>(
        lane < 3u ? lane + 16u : lane);
    const float extra = __shfl_sync(mask, value, fold_source, 32);
    float sum = value;
    if (lane < 3u) {
        sum = __fadd_rn(sum, extra);
    }

    // All-reduce the 16 folded values.
    for (unsigned int distance = 1u; distance <= 8u;
         distance <<= 1u) {
        const int source = static_cast<int>(
            lane < 16u ? (lane ^ distance) : lane);
        const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (lane < 16u) {
            sum = __fadd_rn(sum, other);
        }
    }

    // Three distinct sources deliver results to the extra lanes.
    const int result_source = static_cast<int>(
        lane >= 16u ? lane - 16u : lane);
    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, result_source, 32);
}
```

Compile in a `.cu` file with `nvcc -arch=sm_70` or a newer target. The calling contract is exactly your assumption: physical lanes 0–18 of each participating full warp call this function once, and lanes 19–31 do not call it.

Addition order: first form `a0 = x0 + x16`, `a1 = x1 + x17`, and `a2 = x2 + x18`; the other folded values are `ai = xi`. Then, at distances 1, 2, 4, and 8, each low lane adds its previous-stage sum to its XOR partner’s previous-stage sum, using round-to-nearest float addition. Different lanes can obtain different rounded results because operand ordering differs; every result nevertheless includes every input exactly once. Finite inputs can still overflow during addition.

Exact-once proof: the 16 folded values partition the 19 inputs. Initially each low lane holds one folded value. After distance 1 it holds the two folded values in its aligned pair; after distances 2, 4, and 8 it holds its aligned group of 4, 8, and 16 respectively. At each stage, the XOR partner holds the complementary, disjoint half of that group, so no input is duplicated or omitted. Finally, lanes 16–18 receive the complete results of lanes 0–2 respectively, while lanes 0–15 retain their own complete results.

Source validity and synchronization: the fold reads lanes 16–18 or the caller itself; butterfly partners are always in 0–15, while extra lanes read themselves; the final shuffle reads lanes 0–15. Thus every source participates. All 19 callers execute the same six shuffle instances with the identical mask and width, including lanes that skip an addition. Each caller’s bit is set, and every named lane executes the matching collective, so `__shfl_sync` supplies the required synchronization without implicit lockstep.
